  "answer_text": "Great question! Here's a detailed explanation of why men tend to have larger Adam's apples:\n\n### What Is the Adam's Apple?\n\nThe Adam's apple is a prominence of the thyroid cartilage that partially encloses the larynx (the voice box). Adam's apples are found on both women and men \u2014 they just show up more prominently in men. Also known as the **laryngeal prominence**, the Adam's apple sits right on top of the thyroid gland, so the area is fittingly called the **thyroid cartilage**.\n\n---\n\n### The Role of Puberty and Testosterone\n\nThe Adam's apple is about the same size in people assigned male at birth (AMAB) and those assigned female at birth (AFAB) until puberty. Girls and boys start out with similarly sized thyroid cartilage, but this changes when they hit puberty. The boys' Adam's apples become noticeably bigger and their voices change as their testosterone levels increase.\n\nIn men, the production of testosterone causes the larynx to grow larger, resulting in a deeper voice and a more prominent Adam's apple. Testosterone causes the male larynx to grow significantly during puberty, pushing the thyroid cartilage outward and creating that visible bump on the throat.\n\n---\n\n### Why Does a Larger Larynx = Deeper Voice?\n\nOnce puberty starts, hormones (especially testosterone) may cause the vocal cords to grow bigger. The thyroid cartilage may therefore stick out a bit more to protect these larger vocal cords. People with a larger Adam's apple tend to have a deeper voice than people with a smaller one.\n\n---\n\n### Shape Differences Also Play a Role\n\nThere is also a difference in the **shape** of the laryngeal prominence in males and females. In males, the sides of the laryngeal prominence protrude at an almost 90\u00b0 angle, while in females, it is at a more obtuse 120\u00b0 angle, making it appear less visible.\n\n---\n\n### Do Women Have Adam's Apples?\n\nYes, women do have Adam's apples, but it is usually much less prominent than in men. Both men and women have the same structures in their throats, including the thyroid cartilage, but in women, it tends to be smaller and less noticeable. Girls also undergo changes to their voice box during puberty, but the degree of laryngeal growth in females isn't as significant as in males, so most women don't have a visible Adam's apple.\n\n---\n\n### Evolutionary Theory\n\nResearchers have proposed that a larger larynx originally allowed individuals to produce vocalizations that mimic a larger body size. In humans, a deeper, more resonant voice may have helped adult males sound more imposing, giving them an edge in social competition.\n\nIn summary, the larger Adam's apple in men is primarily a result of **testosterone-driven growth** of the larynx during puberty, which also produces deeper voices as a side effect.",
